EXT. FIELD KITCHEN - ROADSIDE ORCHARD - DUSK

1944. Apple trees blown to splinters. A canvas tent pitched against a stone wall. Soup bubbles in a great steel cauldron. Smoke drifts across a quiet road. Distant guns have gone silent.

SERGEANT MAEVE O'DWYER, 40s, a stout cook in an apron over her tunic, ladles broth into tin cups. She hums.

CORPORAL TOBIAS FINCH, 20s, a lanky private-turned-corporal, sits on a crate, unlacing a boot. He wears three days of beard.

PRIVATE HANNAH BRISCOE, 20s, a signaller, sits cross-legged in the grass with a field telephone in her lap, staring at it.

LIEUTENANT RAFAEL CORTEZ, 30s, young and polished, stands at the tent flap, map in hand, for once without a plan.

CAPTAIN ISABELLE DUVAL, 40s, the company commander, sits on an upturned bucket, quietly drinking.

O'DWYER
Soup's ready, sir.

CORTEZ
Thank you, Sergeant.

O'DWYER
It's mostly potato.

CORTEZ
It's perfect.

He takes the cup. Sips. Closes his eyes.

BRISCOE
Sir, line's dead.

CORTEZ
Of course it is.

BRISCOE
Does that mean the war's over?

DUVAL
It means the line's dead, Private.

A pause.

FINCH
Captain, can I ask something?

DUVAL
Ask.

FINCH
Are we going home?

DUVAL
Eventually.

FINCH
That's not an answer.

DUVAL
It's the only one I've got.

Finch holds out his cup.

FINCH
Sergeant, is there more?

O'DWYER
There's always more.

She fills it. A small smile crosses his face.

Down the road, an engine. A lone jeep, headlights dimmed. Everyone straightens. Rifles lift.

A DISPATCH RIDER, 20s, in a mud-caked coat, pulls up and cuts the engine. He stumbles off, hands raised.

RIDER
Don't shoot. Message from division.

CORTEZ
Read it.

RIDER
It's over, sir. Signed this morning.

Nobody moves. A long breath goes round the company. Finch lowers his boot. Briscoe drops the telephone.

DUVAL
(softly)
Well.

O'DWYER
Soup, sir?

The Rider blinks.

RIDER
Yes, ma'am. Please.
